 3. Hugging Face debuts &quot;Microduck,&quot; a $399 robot 
 
 By Ina Fried 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Microduck. Image: Hugging Face

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Hugging Face today announced " Microduck ," a small, armless robot capable of waddling adorably and picking up small objects with an articulated beak that doubles as a gripper.

 Why it matters: The move is an effort to put a friendly, approachable face on a robotics field dominated by attack dogs and humanoids.

 Driving the news: The $399 duck-like robot comes in several colors and is packed with sensors including an eye-like camera, speaker, microphone, WiFi, Bluetooth, and a small lidar sensor.

 Microduck will come with several preset behaviors, such as being able to follow a laser pointer. In sample videos, Hugging Face showed Microduck picking up a dirty sock, rollerblading and recovering from falls and efforts to push it off balance. However, the target audience is AI developers who can teach the robot new tricks. The company aims to deliver the first robots before Christmas, with preorders starting today. What they're saying: Hugging Face CEO Clem Delangue told Axios it's the first affordable AI robot that one can teach via reinforcement learning, aka trial and error.

 Delangue said the team designed Microduck to be "made to move, ready to fall." "We want these robots to also teach people that robots can fail and make mistakes — and you want to take that into account in your design," he said. Zoom out: Hugging Face became a household name when the company's systems were breached by OpenAI. (See above.) It has also emerged as a central place to find all the world's open-source models.

